## [ISSUE] Backup-code login can bypass the password check

**Severity:** HIGH — email plus one leaked backup code is enough to log in; the password is never checked.

### The Problem
The password check runs only when `totpCode` is empty:
```ts
// line 121
if (user.password && !credentials.totpCode) {
  const isCorrectPassword = await verifyPassword(credentials.password, user.password);
  ...
}
// line 131 (new)
if (user.twoFactorEnabled && credentials.backupCode) {
  ... indexOf(credentials.backupCode) ... // success -> falls through to return user
} else if (user.twoFactorEnabled) {
  ... TOTP check ...
}
```
If a client calls `POST /api/auth/callback/credentials` with `email`, `totpCode=x`, `backupCode=<valid>` and any `password`, then:
1. line 121 is skipped because `totpCode` is truthy;
2. line 131 is taken because `backupCode` is truthy, so the dummy `totpCode` is never checked;
3. the backup code matches and `authorize` returns the user.

### Theory / Proof
The `!credentials.totpCode` guard exists for the SAML/external-IdP TOTP flow (`totpEmail`), where no password is sent. Before this PR, anyone who skipped the password still had to pass the TOTP check in the `twoFactorEnabled` branch. The new branch is checked first and accepts a backup code in place of that check, so a backup code can now stand in for both factors at once. NIST SP 800-63B §5.1.2 treats look-up secrets (backup codes) as a single "something you have" factor. They must not replace the memorized secret.

### What happens today vs. what will break
The UI never sends both fields, because "Lost access" clears `totpCode`. A hand-made request does. Backup codes are usually printed or saved as `cal-backup-codes.txt`, so they leak more easily than a password. With this bug, one leaked code is enough to take over the account.

### Research
GitHub, Google and Okta always ask for the password first and use recovery codes only as the second step. None of them lets a recovery code stand in for the password.

### Recommendation
Always verify the password for CAL-provider users when the backup-code path is used, independent of `totpCode`:
```ts
if (user.password && (!credentials.totpCode || credentials.backupCode)) {
  const isCorrectPassword = await verifyPassword(credentials.password, user.password);
  if (!isCorrectPassword) throw new Error(ErrorCode.IncorrectEmailPassword);
}
```
Better still, tie the password skip to the external-IdP case (`user.identityProvider !== IdentityProvider.CAL`) and not to whether `totpCode` is present.

## [RACE CONDITION] Backup codes are not single-use under concurrency

**Severity:** MEDIUM — the same backup code can be used for several logins at once, and a used code can come back.

### The Problem
```ts
// read (line ~74): select backupCodes
const backupCodes = JSON.parse(symmetricDecrypt(user.backupCodes, KEY));
const index = backupCodes.indexOf(code);
if (index === -1) throw ...;
backupCodes[index] = null;                     // line 148
await prisma.user.update({ where: { id: user.id }, data: { backupCodes: symmetricEncrypt(...) } });
```
The `findUnique` read and the `update` write are separate statements with no transaction, no row lock, and no compare-and-swap on the old value.

### Theory / Proof
- **Replay:** requests A and B both send code `c`. Both read the same ciphertext, both find `c`, both write. Both logins succeed.
- **Lost update:** A uses `c1` and B uses `c2` at the same time. A writes `[null, c2, ...]` and B writes `[c1, null, ...]`. Whichever write lands last wins, so `c1` or `c2` becomes valid again.
Postgres runs this at READ COMMITTED by default, and a plain `UPDATE ... SET backupCodes = $1` does not check that the row still holds the value that was read.

### What happens today vs. what will break
A single user clicking once never hits this. An attacker who has one code can send several requests in parallel. The per-email rate limit (`checkRateLimitAndThrowError`) allows a few requests in a burst, so the "each code works once" promise breaks.

### Research
OWASP's MFA Cheat Sheet says recovery codes must be single-use. The usual fix is an atomic conditional update, or a table with one row per code that is deleted with `DELETE ... RETURNING`.

### Recommendation
Make the write conditional on the ciphertext that was read, and fail if nothing changed:
```ts
const { count } = await prisma.user.updateMany({
  where: { id: user.id, backupCodes: user.backupCodes },
  data: { backupCodes: symmetricEncrypt(JSON.stringify(backupCodes), KEY) },
});
if (count === 0) throw new Error(ErrorCode.IncorrectBackupCode);
```
Another option is to store hashed codes in a separate `BackupCode` table and use them with `deleteMany({ where: { userId, codeHash } })`, checking `count === 1`.

## [ISSUE] Dismissing the backup-codes step leaves the UI out of sync with the server

**Severity:** MEDIUM — 2FA is on, but the settings page shows it as off, and the modal state is left stale.

### The Problem
`handleEnable` now moves to `SetupStep.DisplayBackupCodes` after `/totp/enable` returns 200. At that point `twoFactorEnabled` is already `true` in the database. `onEnable()` (which calls `utils.viewer.me.invalidate()` in `pages/settings/security/two-factor-auth.tsx`) and `resetState()` are called only from the new "Close" button:
```tsx
<Dialog open={open} onOpenChange={onOpenChange}>   // line 166
```
Radix `Dialog` calls `onOpenChange(false)` when the user presses Escape or clicks the overlay. The page's handler only runs `setEnableModalOpen(!enableModalOpen)`.

### What happens today vs. what will break
Before this PR, `onEnable()` ran as soon as enable succeeded, so any way of closing the dialog was fine. Now, if the user presses Escape on the backup-codes screen:
- `viewer.me` is not invalidated, so the switch and badge still say "Disabled" while 2FA is on;
- `step` stays `DisplayBackupCodes`, so the next time the switch is clicked the Enable modal opens straight to the old codes;
- the codes stay in component state and in the blob URL.
A user who thinks 2FA is off may be confused or may not know that 2FA is protecting the account. If they click the switch again, the modal reopens on the old backup codes and not on the setup flow.

### Research
Radix Dialog docs: `onOpenChange` is called on Escape (`onEscapeKeyDown`) and on outside pointer-down (`onPointerDownOutside`) unless those events are prevented.

### Recommendation
Treat any close on this step as completion:
```tsx
<Dialog
  open={open}
  onOpenChange={(isOpen) => {
    if (!isOpen && step === SetupStep.DisplayBackupCodes) {
      resetState();
      onEnable();
      return;
    }
    onOpenChange();
  }}>
```
Another option is to call `onEnable`'s invalidation right after the enable succeeds and use the backup-codes step only for display.

## [ISSUE] Backup-code blob URL and state are never released

**Severity:** LOW — a small resource leak, and the plaintext secrets stay in memory longer than they need to.

### The Problem
```ts
const resetState = () => {      // line 71
  setPassword("");
  setErrorMessage(null);
  setStep(SetupStep.ConfirmPassword);
};
```
`URL.revokeObjectURL(backupCodesUrl)` runs only when `handleSetup` runs again (line 98). Nothing revokes it on Close, Cancel or unmount, and `backupCodes` / `backupCodesUrl` state is never cleared. `pages/settings/security/two-factor-auth.tsx` keeps `EnableTwoFactorModal` mounted for the whole life of the page and only toggles `open`, so the plaintext codes stay in React state and the Blob stays in memory until the page unloads.

### Theory / Proof
Per the File API spec, a blob URL keeps its Blob alive until `revokeObjectURL` is called or the document unloads. Cal.com is a single-page app, so that can take a long time. The direct exposure is small: after reset, the `<a href>` is no longer rendered and blob URLs cannot be listed. This is a hygiene issue for secret material, not an exploitable leak.

### Recommendation
```ts
const resetState = () => {
  setPassword("");
  setErrorMessage(null);
  setBackupCodes([]);
  if (backupCodesUrl) URL.revokeObjectURL(backupCodesUrl);
  setBackupCodesUrl("");
  setStep(SetupStep.ConfirmPassword);
};
useEffect(() => () => { if (backupCodesUrl) URL.revokeObjectURL(backupCodesUrl); }, [backupCodesUrl]);
```
